      "writtenOn": "2021-07-19T20:42:23Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "We should assert that we aren\u0027t double-rewinding or double-accepting in accept() and rewind(), since that would break us (leaving the error reporter null forever). \n\n    SkASSERTF(fErrorReporter, \"Checkpoint was already accepted/rewound\");\n\n\nCuriosity: why do we do it this way instead of using `setErrorCount` to roll back speculative error reports? I\u0027m not sure why the old way wasn\u0027t sufficient.",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 348,
        "startChar": 7,
        "endLine": 354,
        "endChar": 28
      },
      "revId": "7bc300ca72107799407460bbbe595b2d4357dc3a",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "c29c7b3b_73bdb935",
        "filename": "src/sksl/SkSLDSLParser.h",
        "patchSetId": 52
      },
      "lineNbr": 362,
      "author": {
        "id": 11059
      },
      "writtenOn": "2021-07-19T20:42:23Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "The Checkpoint might potentially be accepted even if an error occurred:\n\n    Checkpoint x; /* error occurs */; x.accept();\n\n\nIt seems as if this is just dropping the errors, on (I guess) the assumption that we would only accept a result with no errors. The original intent of the SkSLParser\u0027s Checkpoint class was that you could always accept or rewind the result, whether or not the result was an error. I\u0027m not sure if we actually use that flexibility today, but I\u0027d be disappointed to lose the option.",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 360,
        "startChar": 1,
        "endLine": 362,
        "endChar": 9
      },
      "revId": "7bc300ca72107799407460bbbe595b2d4357dc3a",
